In Android, you can achieve this using the `postDelayedWithExecutor` method, which was introduced in Android 4.7. This method allows you to post a Runnable to a Handler with a specified delay. Here's how you can use it:

```java
Handler handler = new Handler(Looper.getMainLooper());
handler.postDelayedWithExecutor(new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        DoSomething();
    }
}, 5000);
```

In this code, `postDelayedWithExecutor` will call the `run` method after a delay of 5000 milliseconds (5 seconds), which in turn will call your `DoSomething` method. Note that the delay is specified in milliseconds.

Alternatively, you can use a ScheduledExecutorService, which is a more modern approach. You can create a ScheduledExecutorService and schedule a Runnable to run with a specified delay using the `scheduleAtFixedRateWithInitialDelay` method. This was introduced in Java 11.4 as part of the Android SDK.

```java
ScheduledExecutorService executor = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1);
executor.scheduleAtFixedRateWithInitialDelay(new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        DoSomething();
    }
}, 5000, 0, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);
```

In this code, the `scheduleAtFixedRateWithInitialDelay` method will run the `run` method after an initial delay of 5000 milliseconds, and then not repeat.

